---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Sideboard Strategy & Principles - Replenish Guide"
    image="/src/assets/banners/flooded.jpeg"
    description="Premodern Replenish sideboard theory: how to pivot into a midrange beatdown deck, decide what to trim, and build your 15-card sideboard."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Strategy & Principles"
        description="Winning post-board games comes down to reading your role, knowing what to cut, and building the right 15 cards."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/flooded.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Flooded Strand"
        imageCaption="Flooded Strand by Rob Alexander"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Building Your 15" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="mt-0">
            Don't build your sideboard from scratch on tournament morning.
            Before a big event, start from a pool of 85–90 cards you trust,
            then cut down from there based on what you expect to face
            locally.
        </p>
        <ol class="pl-md">
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Start Broad:</strong> Keep a wide pool of 85–90 cards,
                covering archetype options and specific bullets.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Assess the Metagame:</strong> A field full of red and aggressive
                tribal decks calls for board sweepers (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law"
                    >Sphere of Law</a
                >). A field full of blue permission decks calls for mana taxers (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                >) and solid midrange threats.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Prioritize Utility Slots:</strong> Rank your flex cards by raw
                power and how many matchups they help in.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Cut Strategically:</strong> Drop narrow bullets that
                don't earn their slot in at least two important matchups.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Lock In Transformation Packages:</strong> Only keep a post-board
                pivot if it flips a bad Game 1 into a good one against several
                common decks.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-0">
                <strong>Main vs. Side Positioning:</strong> Decide what goes maindeck
                versus sideboard based on how often you expect each matchup.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <strong>The Creature Sideboard Package:</strong> <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                >Meddling Mage</a
            > let you turn into an aggressive midrange deck after
            boarding. That puts opponents in a bind: keep all their anti-enchantment
            and graveyard hate and they get beaten down by fliers; cut some hate to
            respect your creatures and they get run over by the combo.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Transforming & Reading Your Role" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 class="mt-0">Transforming Against Control</h3>
        <p>
            Against heavy blue permission decks, you change what the deck is.
            Card-draw engines like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            >, reactive pieces like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            >, and extra spot removal come out. In come <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Abeyance"
                >Abeyance</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                >Arcane Denial</a
            >, and a full set of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            >. Turning into a proactive midrange beatdown deck throws off everything
            they boarded in expecting a slow combo game.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            Keeping the Combo Against Aggro
        </h3>
        <p>
            Against linear aggro decks, leave the combo engine fully intact so
            you can race. Just swap out situational interaction—for example, trim <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > when the opponent has no enchantments or artifacts worth hitting.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            Retiring Mages Against Green and Midrange
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            Against heavy green midrange and attrition decks (The Rock, RG Ponza,
            Terrageddon), <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > comes out. Their creatures outclass a 2/2, and their plan rarely hinges
            on one specific spell you can name. Bring in <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > instead to take over combat and the life race.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Key Cards & Trimming Rules" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 class="mt-0">Exalted Angel is the Universal Default</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            > comes in against just about every non-combo opponent—aggro, control, and
            midrange alike. The only time it stays out is against fast combo decks
            (Enchantress, the mirror, Elves), where slowing down your own combo
            is fatal. If you're not sure what a non-combo opponent is doing, bringing
            in Angel is almost always right.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Frantic Search is the First Trim</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search"
                >Frantic Search</a
            > is usually the first trim against blue control and discard-heavy
            decks, where losing a card on resolution (-1 on cast) hurts too much.
            Keep the full set against fast aggro and combo, where getting going
            on turn three is everything.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            Core Trimming Rules Post-Board
        </h3>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                <strong>Against Blue Control:</strong> Trim <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > so they can't get easy 2-for-1s with their counters.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                <strong>Against Non-Creature Shells:</strong> Cut some <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                >. Be careful, though—a lot of control pilots bring in
                creature clocks (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                    >Exalted Angel</a
                >) after board, so keep 1 or 2
                removal spells around.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-0">
                <strong>Against Red Blast Suites (Sligh, Ponza):</strong> Trim expensive
                or card-negative blue engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > so you don't hand them huge tempo trades with one-mana blasts
                like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Pyroblast"
                    >Pyroblast</a
                > or Red Elemental Blast.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>
</Layout>
